There was an Apache-2.0 window on 22 August 2026, and it cannot be closed.

Recorded because it is the kind of fact that has to be findable afterwards, and
because pretending otherwise would be the same mistake in the other direction.

Commit 656bc88 relicensed this project to Apache-2.0 and was pushed to the
public repository at approximately 08:58 UTC on 22 August 2026. Commit 67a85b8
was the last under those terms. The licence was reverted to AGPL-3.0-or-later
about eighty minutes later.

The reason for the revert is that the relicensing was aimed at a scenario the
project is not pursuing — a third party embedding this in a closed product. The
route actually taken is that the maintainer operates the service, which the
Affero clause does not obstruct at all. A licence changed for a plan that
changed should change back.

What matters is the part that does not revert. **Apache-2.0 is permissive, and
that grant is irrevocable for the commits it covered.** Anyone who obtained a
copy of the repository between those two points holds an Apache-2.0 licence to
that code, permanently, including the right to use it in a closed product
without publishing their changes. Reverting the licence changes what happens
next; it cannot reach back.

The window was short and the exposure is therefore small, but "small" is not
"none" and this file is not the place to round it down.

